WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.210
- So it's possible that a
lot of President Trump's

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.360
late term pardons might not actually

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:05.980
shield the people that he pardoned.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.330
We know that the Trump administration

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:08.700
was perfectly capable of writing

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.210
the broadest possible pardons.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.450
For example, with Michael Flynn,

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:14.560
he received a pardon on
"any and all possible crimes

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:17.150
arising out of the facts,"
for which he was charged.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.530
You rarely see pardons like that.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:20.890
It hearkens back to Gerald
Ford pardoning Richard Nixon.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.410
It's extremely rare, but
they know how to do it.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.200
The thing is, the Trump administration

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.560
barely followed any procedure whatsoever,

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.410
so it's not surprising
that some of these pardons

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.230
might be defective,
like the pardons against

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.570
Bannon, Manafort, Stone, Papadopoulos.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.880
These might actually not
provide any protection

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:36.920
for these reprobates.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.590
All of these pardons were
drawn incredibly narrowly,

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.880
and prosecutors might be
able to charge other crimes.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.353
I'll explain why in part two.

